,1],\"automargin\":true,\"type\":\"linear\",\"autorange\":true,\"range\":[2.2878359000000001,6.4682781],\"tickmode\":\"auto\",\"ticktext\":[\"3\",\"4\",\"5\",\"6\"],\"tickvals\":[3,4,5,6],\"categoryorder\":\"array\",\"categoryarray\":[\"3\",\"4\",\"5\",\"6\"],\"nticks\":null,\"ticks\":\"\",\"tickcolor\":null,\"ticklen\":3.9850560398505608,\"tickwidth\":0,\"showticklabels\":true,\"tickfont\":{\"color\":\"#E8DDD0\",\"family\":\"\",\"size\":12.7521793275218},\"tickangle\":-0,\"showline\":false,\"linecolor\":\"#555566\",\"linewidth\":0,\"showgrid\":true,\"gridcolor\":\"#555566\",\"gridwidth\":0.5313408053134081,\"zeroline\":false,\"anchor\":\"x\",\"title\":{\"text\":\"Unemployment rate (%)\",\"font\":{\"color\":\"rgba(232,221,208,1)\",\"family\":\"\",\"size\":15.940224159402243}},\"hoverformat\":\".2f\",\"zerolinecolor\":\"#555566\"},\"shapes\":[],\"showlegend\":false,\"legend\":{\"bgcolor\":null,\"bordercolor\":null,\"borderwidth\":0,\"font\":{\"color\":\"#E8DDD0\",\"family\":\"\",\"size\":12.7521793275218},\"title\":{\"text\":\"\",\"font\":{\"color\":\"rgba(232,221,208,1)\",\"family\":\"\",\"size\":15.940224159402243}}},\"hovermode\":\"closest\",\"barmode\":\"relative\"},\"config\":{\"doubleClick\":\"reset\",\"modeBarButtonsToAdd\":[\"hoverclosest\",\"hovercompare\"],\"showSendToCloud\":false},\"source\":\"A\",\"attrs\":{\"89bc5b711f96\":{\"x\":{},\"y\":{},\"type\":\"scatter\"}},\"cur_data\":\"89bc5b711f96\",\"visdat\":{\"89bc5b711f96\":[\"function (y) \",\"x\"]},\"highlight\":{\"on\":\"plotly_click\",\"persistent\":false,\"dynamic\":false,\"selectize\":false,\"opacityDim\":0.20000000000000001,\"selected\":{\"opacity\":1},\"debounce\":0},\"shinyEvents\":[\"plotly_hover\",\"plotly_click\",\"plotly_selected\",\"plotly_relayout\",\"plotly_brushed\",\"plotly_brushing\",\"plotly_clickannotation\",\"plotly_doubleclick\",\"plotly_deselect\",\"plotly_afterplot\",\"plotly_sunburstclick\"],\"base_url\":\"https://plot.ly\"},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nNote the sharp spike around 2020 Q2 — the COVID-19 shock. Keep this in mind when interpreting model diagnostics and accuracy.\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\ntheme_narsil()\nunemp |> gg_season(unemp)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](ex_module2_full_review_files/figure-html/season-subseries-1.png){width=672}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nunemp |> gg_subseries(unemp)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](ex_module2_full_review_files/figure-html/subseries-1.png){width=672}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\ntheme_narsil()\nunemp |>\n  gg_tsdisplay(unemp, plot_type = \"partial\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](ex_module2_full_review_files/figure-html/tsdisplay-1.png){width=672}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {.callout-note appearance=\"simple\"}\n## What to look for\n\n- **Trend**: Is there a long-run level shift, or does the series return to a stable mean after shocks?\n- **Seasonality**: Is there a repeating quarterly pattern? How stable is it across years?\n- **ACF/PACF**: What correlation structure would you expect in the residuals? Does the series look stationary?\n:::\n\n## Train/Test Split\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nh <- 8 # 2 years ahead\n\nunemp_train <- unemp |> slice_head(n = nrow(unemp) - h)\nunemp_test  <- unemp |> slice_tail(n = h)\n```\n:::\n\n\nThe test set covers the most recent 8 quarters (2 years). All model fitting is done on `unemp_train`; accuracy is evaluated against the full `unemp` tsibble.\n\n---\n\n# Exercise\n\nThis is a full-pipeline exercise. Work through each section in order — later sections depend on the objects you build earlier.\n\n## Exercise 1 — Module 1 baseline\n\nRe-estimate the Module 1 baseline model on `unemp_train`. This is your benchmark: every model you build in this exercise must be compared against it.\n\nThe baseline uses:\n\n- STL decomposition (choose sensible `trend(window)` and `season(window)` values for a quarterly series)\n- Drift on the seasonally adjusted component\n- SNAIVE on the seasonal component\n\nSave the spec as `baseline_spec` and fit it inside a `mable` called `unemp_fit`. Then:\n\na. Produce forecasts for `h = 8` quarters.\nb. Plot the forecast (overlay, `level = NULL`).\nc. Inspect residual diagnostics with `gg_tsresiduals()`. Do the residuals look like white noise? Is the COVID spike visible?\nd. Compute test-set accuracy using `accuracy(unemp_fc, unemp)`.\n\n::: {.callout-tip}\n## Hint\nRemember that `accuracy()` requires the **full** tsibble — not just the test set — so that MASE and RMSSE denominators are computed correctly.\n:::\n\n## Exercise 2 — ETS models\n\nAdd at least two ETS-based approaches to `unemp_fit`:\n\na. **STL + ETS**: Use `decomposition_model()` with an ETS model on `season_adjust`. Suppress seasonality in the ETS spec (`season(\"N\")`). The seasonal component defaults to SNAIVE.\nb. **Full ETS**: Fit `ETS(unemp)` directly, without explicit decomposition. Let R select the best error/trend/season structure automatically.\n\nFor each model:\n\n- Add it to the same `mable` as the baseline (a single `model()` call with all specs).\n- Check residual diagnostics for the STL + ETS model.\n- Include both in the accuracy comparison at the end.\n\n## Exercise 3 — ARIMA and SARIMA\n\nAdd at least two ARIMA-based approaches:\n\na. **STL + ARIMA**: Use `decomposition_model()` with `ARIMA(season_adjust ~ PDQ(0,0,0))`. The seasonal component defaults to SNAIVE.\nb. **Automatic SARIMA**: Fit a full seasonal ARIMA directly — `ARIMA(unemp, stepwise = FALSE, approximation = FALSE)` — without explicit decomposition.\n\nFor each model:\n\n- Add it to the same `mable`.\n- Check residual diagnostics for the STL + ARIMA model.\n- Include both in the accuracy comparison.\n\n::: {.callout-note appearance=\"simple\"}\n## Identifying a manual SARIMA candidate (optional stretch)\n\nUse `gg_tsdisplay()` on the differenced series to identify a candidate ARIMA order manually, then add it as a third model (`sarima_manual`). Compare it against the automatic selection.\n:::\n\n## Exercise 4 — Mixed decomposition models\n\nThis section connects directly to the [Modular Forecasting](../../modules/module_2/04_modular_fcst/modular_forecasting.qmd) class document.\n\nAdd the two mixed combinations to your `mable`:\n\na. **STL + ETS + ARIMA**: ETS models the seasonally adjusted component (`season_adjust`); ARIMA models the seasonal component (`season_year`).\nb. **STL + ARIMA + ETS**: ARIMA models the seasonally adjusted component (`season_adjust ~ PDQ(0,0,0)`); ETS models the seasonal component (`season_year ~ trend(\"N\")`).\n\nRecall the constraint logic for each component:\n\n| Component | What it contains | What to suppress |\n|:--|:--|:--|\n| `season_adjust` | trend + cycle + remainder | seasonality |\n| `season_year` | seasonal pattern only | trend |\n\nAfter fitting:\n\n- Produce a single forecast plot with all models overlaid (`level = NULL`).\n- Compute a final accuracy table with all models, sorted by RMSE.\n- Answer: which approach performs best on this series? Does the winner make intuitive sense given what you observed in the EDA?\n\n## Exercise 5 — Reflection\n\nWrite a short paragraph (5–8 sentences) addressing the following:\n\na. The COVID-19 spike in 2020 Q2 is a clear outlier. How does it affect model diagnostics and accuracy metrics? Which models appear most sensitive to it?\nb. The baseline was designed in Module 1 for a different series (`mexretail`). Is it still a competitive benchmark here, or does the unemployment series favor a different model family? What characteristics of the series drive your answer?\nc. Would you expect the same model ranking to hold on a different country's unemployment series? What would you check first before deciding?\n",
